 summary: [
  ["Adolescence", "Adolescence is the period of change from childhood to adulthood. It generally begins around 10 years of age and usually lasts until 19 years. Life’s stages are infancy, childhood, adolescence, adulthood and old age."],
  ["Many kinds of change", "Adolescence brings physical changes (height, weight, body shape, voice, hair, pimples), internal changes, and emotional and behavioural changes. Their timing, nature and extent vary from person to person, and this is normal."],
  ["Secondary sexual characteristics and puberty", "Features that help distinguish males from females but are not directly involved in reproduction, like a hoarse voice and facial hair in boys and the development of breasts in girls, are secondary sexual characteristics. They mark the onset of puberty, when the body changes outside and inside to become an adult capable of reproduction."],
  ["The menstrual cycle", "In girls, adolescence is marked by the start of the menstrual cycle, which generally recurs every 28–30 days (21–35 days in many healthy girls). Menstruation, the blood discharge, lasts 3–7 days and stops naturally by about 45–55 years. It is natural; myths such as isolating girls have no scientific basis."],
  ["Emotions and behaviour", "Adolescents may have mood swings and stronger emotions. Channelled well, through music, dance, sports or social work, these lead to creativity and compassion. Understanding our emotions helps us choose thoughtfully."],
  ["Staying healthy", "A balanced diet (calcium for bones, proteins for growth, iron for blood, plus carbohydrates, fats, vitamins and minerals), personal and menstrual hygiene, and regular physical activity keep adolescents healthy. Government schemes support nutrition and menstrual hygiene."],
  ["Being safe online and offline", "Be polite, respectful and responsible in person and on social media. Cyberbullying, meaning harassment through digital devices, should be handled wisely by seeking help from parents and teachers. Protect privacy and think before posting."],
  ["Say NO to harmful substances", "Tobacco, gutka, beedi, cigarettes, alcohol and drugs are addictive and harm body and mind, for example by damaging the lungs. Say NO the first time and every time. Help is available through family, counselling and the helpline 14446."],
  ["Hormones: the ‘why’", "The changes of adolescence are mainly controlled by hormones: chemicals produced in the body and released on signals from the brain. They regulate growth and development and influence mood and behaviour."]
 ]
